WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.510
ED GLAESER: Boston was never a city of kings or conquerors.

00:00:03.510 --> 00:00:07.140
The city's tallest building long bore the name of a smuggler, John Hancock.

00:00:07.140 --> 00:00:10.380
By the 19th century, Boston was trumpeting its intellectual ambitions

00:00:10.380 --> 00:00:11.940
as the Athens of America.

00:00:11.940 --> 00:00:16.079
But all that learning was built on the wealth of a solid mercantile base.

00:00:16.079 --> 00:00:20.080
During the 20th century, America started to see the rise of consumer cities.

00:00:20.080 --> 00:00:22.470
Places like Los Angeles or Miami that grew

00:00:22.470 --> 00:00:26.250
because people wanted to live there, not because firms had any particular reason

00:00:26.250 --> 00:00:27.870
to choose that spot.

00:00:27.870 --> 00:00:31.920
In a sense, Boston was America's first consumer city.

00:00:31.920 --> 00:00:35.340
The colonists who came to Massachusetts during the 1620s, and to Boston

00:00:35.340 --> 00:00:39.514
after 1630, were motivated by lifestyle choices more than economics.

00:00:39.514 --> 00:00:41.430
They wanted to live in a Puritan society where

00:00:41.430 --> 00:00:44.022
they would be free from persecution by English authorities.

00:00:44.022 --> 00:00:46.230
And would, of, course be free to persecute others who

00:00:46.230 --> 00:00:48.270
didn't conform to their own godly ways.

00:00:48.270 --> 00:00:51.030
They wanted Boston to shine as a city upon a hill,

00:00:51.030 --> 00:00:54.420
as John Winthrop preached aboard the Arbella on his way to the city.

00:00:54.420 --> 00:00:56.820
I recognize that I'm displaying the standard economists

00:00:56.820 --> 00:01:00.470
reductionism by referring to the desire for eternal salvation as consumption.

00:01:00.470 --> 00:01:03.460
But, well, it certainly wasn't production.

00:01:03.460 --> 00:01:06.330
Boston had essentially no natural economic advantages,

00:01:06.330 --> 00:01:10.680
except for access to abundant cod, and some pretty great cranberry bogs.

00:01:10.680 --> 00:01:13.380
Consequently, the first few decades of Boston's existence

00:01:13.380 --> 00:01:16.500
were spent trying to find a sustainable economic model.

00:01:16.500 --> 00:01:19.320
For the Bostonians needed to import English manufactured goods.

00:01:19.320 --> 00:01:21.450
They needed guns, they needed bibles, and they

00:01:21.450 --> 00:01:25.110
didn't have all that much that was worth shipping back across the Atlantic.

00:01:25.110 --> 00:01:27.150
Boston didn't have gold and silver deposits

00:01:27.150 --> 00:01:29.070
like the Spanish colonies of the south.

00:01:29.070 --> 00:01:32.207
New England's climate is a bit too much like the climate of old England,

00:01:32.207 --> 00:01:34.290
which meant that Massachusetts couldn't grow crops

00:01:34.290 --> 00:01:37.620
like sugar that were valuable, because they were rare back in Europe.

00:01:37.620 --> 00:01:41.100
In the early days, Boston and Massachusetts functioned as something

00:01:41.100 --> 00:01:43.350
like a colonial era Ponzi scheme.

00:01:43.350 --> 00:01:47.550
Which is appropriate enough, since Ponzi himself was also a Bostonian.

00:01:47.550 --> 00:01:50.550
Each new wave of settlers brought with them a bit of gold.

00:01:50.550 --> 00:01:53.940
The old settlers then sold food and land to the new settlers,

00:01:53.940 --> 00:01:58.350
and used the cash that was paid to then pay for English manufactured goods.

00:01:58.350 --> 00:02:01.230
But like all Ponzi schemes, this one couldn't go on forever,

00:02:01.230 --> 00:02:04.980
and immigration to Massachusetts slow dramatically after 1640.

00:02:04.980 --> 00:02:07.710
The chaos of the English Civil War slowed the migration,

00:02:07.710 --> 00:02:10.289
and Cromwell's victory made it unnecessary to cross

00:02:10.288 --> 00:02:13.410
the fierce Atlantic to enjoy the pleasures of banning makeup

00:02:13.410 --> 00:02:15.570
and restricting Christmas celebrations.

00:02:15.570 --> 00:02:18.450
So Bostonians needed a new business model.

00:02:18.450 --> 00:02:20.340
They tried a number of different approaches.

00:02:20.340 --> 00:02:22.440
John Winthrop's son founded an iron works

00:02:22.440 --> 00:02:25.380
that he hoped would provide a valuable export industry.

00:02:25.380 --> 00:02:26.520
It didn't.

00:02:26.520 --> 00:02:30.360
Boston's economic salvation came from hunger in Barbados.

00:02:30.360 --> 00:02:32.670
The Caribbean colonies were cash cows.

00:02:32.670 --> 00:02:35.040
Europeans would pay a fortune for their sugar.

00:02:35.040 --> 00:02:38.670
But the land became so valuable that it didn't pay to grow food there.

00:02:38.670 --> 00:02:41.850
By 1647, Winthrop had learned that Barbados

00:02:41.850 --> 00:02:45.930
planters where, and I quote, so intent upon planting sugar

00:02:45.930 --> 00:02:49.530
that they had rather buy food at very dear rates than produce it by labor,

00:02:49.530 --> 00:02:52.050
so infinite is the profit of sugar works.

00:02:52.050 --> 00:02:55.800
So starting in the 1640s, a triangular trade began.

00:02:55.800 --> 00:02:59.280
The more tropical colonies would ship sugar and tobacco back to Europe.

00:02:59.280 --> 00:03:03.300
Boston would ship basic commodities, fish, livestock, wood products

00:03:03.300 --> 00:03:05.310
to the southern colonies of the Americas.

00:03:05.310 --> 00:03:07.170
New-Englanders would buy manufactured goods

00:03:07.170 --> 00:03:10.500
with the cash earned by selling to the sugar producing regions.

00:03:10.500 --> 00:03:15.280
As late as 1770, 73% of New England's exports were going to the Caribbean

00:03:15.280 --> 00:03:16.890
and to the southern colonies.

00:03:16.890 --> 00:03:22.980
35% of those exports were fish, 31% were livestock, and 21% was wood related.

00:03:22.980 --> 00:03:26.970
Boston was one hub of that triangle, and also the triangular trade

00:03:26.970 --> 00:03:29.220
and slaves that developed simultaneously.

00:03:29.220 --> 00:03:33.420
For a century, Boston thrived as the preeminent trading hub of North America

00:03:33.420 --> 00:03:38.270
and the largest city in English colonies with 12,000 inhabitants in 1720.

00:03:38.270 --> 00:03:42.150
Boston's merchants were smart, and the city's sailors were skilled.

00:03:42.150 --> 00:03:45.120
But Boston had no natural lock on this export model.

00:03:45.120 --> 00:03:48.030
The cities of Philadelphia and New York were further south.

00:03:48.030 --> 00:03:51.390
They each enjoyed a better river network, and a more fertile hinterland.

00:03:51.390 --> 00:03:55.350
Philadelphia's population surpassed that of Boston after 1740.

00:03:55.350 --> 00:03:57.960
Boston's mid 18th century economic doldrums

00:03:57.960 --> 00:03:59.580
led to some pretty ornery merchants.

00:03:59.580 --> 00:04:02.880
Including John Hancock, who took particular umbrage at England's

00:04:02.880 --> 00:04:05.280
attempts to tax and limit their trade.

00:04:05.280 --> 00:04:07.500
Their unhappiness was one of the contributing factors

00:04:07.500 --> 00:04:09.390
that led to the American Revolution.

00:04:09.390 --> 00:04:13.110
But after that revolution, Boston soared once more, and once again,

00:04:13.110 --> 00:04:17.240
its success reflected its stock of water related human capital.

00:04:17.240 --> 00:04:21.750
The decades after 1790 were an era of early globalization.

00:04:21.750 --> 00:04:24.300
America was freed from trading restrictions,

00:04:24.300 --> 00:04:26.490
and merchants started buying bigger boats that

00:04:26.490 --> 00:04:28.710
were capable of sailing odysseys.

00:04:28.710 --> 00:04:30.810
They traveled to Canton and South Africa.

00:04:30.810 --> 00:04:33.300
They cruised for whales in the Pacific Ocean.

00:04:33.300 --> 00:04:36.240
Boston's slightly more northern location mattered less

00:04:36.240 --> 00:04:39.120
when the ships were traveling such long distances.

00:04:39.120 --> 00:04:42.240
Between 1790 and 1840, Boston's population

00:04:42.240 --> 00:04:45.360
increased from 18,000 to 90,000.

00:04:45.360 --> 00:04:49.050
And its economic strength was in the sea, no manufacturing.

00:04:49.050 --> 00:04:52.320
As late as 1840, Boston had 10,000 workers

00:04:52.320 --> 00:04:56.080
in the maritime trades and only 5,000 manufacturing,

00:04:56.080 --> 00:04:59.070
which was less than neighboring Lowell, one center of Massachusetts

00:04:59.070 --> 00:05:00.460
textile industry.

00:05:00.460 --> 00:05:03.610
Boston still shows the legacy of its commercial strength

00:05:03.610 --> 00:05:05.410
during the era of sail.

00:05:05.410 --> 00:05:08.200
The wharves that still stand, most notably the Long Wharf

00:05:08.200 --> 00:05:11.050
itself were the centers of commerce during that era.

00:05:11.050 --> 00:05:14.890
Small houses close to the port, like the still standing home of Paul Revere,

00:05:14.890 --> 00:05:17.770
were built so that their residents could walk to the wharves.

00:05:17.770 --> 00:05:20.860
The old statehouse, a site on Boston's Freedom Trail,

00:05:20.860 --> 00:05:23.200
once held a merchants exchange and warehouses.

00:05:23.200 --> 00:05:25.120
State Street, which was once King Street,

00:05:25.120 --> 00:05:27.610
leads right from that building down to the wharf.

00:05:27.610 --> 00:05:31.390
The city's large Irish population is also a gift of Boston's dominance

00:05:31.390 --> 00:05:33.010
during the era of sail.

00:05:33.010 --> 00:05:34.720
Desperate immigrants could sail to Boston

00:05:34.720 --> 00:05:37.570
faster than they could to New York during the 1840s.

00:05:37.570 --> 00:05:40.030
And consequently, Boston became a major port of entry

00:05:40.030 --> 00:05:42.370
for those who were fleeing the potato famine.

00:05:42.370 --> 00:05:45.070
When the era of sail was replaced by the air of steam,

00:05:45.070 --> 00:05:47.926
New York's maritime dominance became almost complete.

00:05:47.926 --> 00:05:50.050
And subsequent immigrants would come overwhelmingly

00:05:50.050 --> 00:05:52.240
to Ellis Island, not Boston.

00:05:52.240 --> 00:05:55.840
As a consequence, New York will become a dizzying ethnic mix,

00:05:55.840 --> 00:06:00.310
while Boston was still dominated by the divide between Irish and Yankee.

00:06:00.310 --> 00:06:02.620
Boston's merchants did many great things.

00:06:02.620 --> 00:06:04.660
They fought for liberty, which is so valuable

00:06:04.660 --> 00:06:06.255
for their financial livelihoods.

00:06:06.255 --> 00:06:08.380
They built the infrastructure, including rail lines

00:06:08.380 --> 00:06:12.010
that would help Boston reinvent itself once again during the Industrial Era.

00:06:12.010 --> 00:06:14.920
They invested in colleges and universities.

00:06:14.920 --> 00:06:17.570
After World War II, those academic institutions,

00:06:17.570 --> 00:06:21.100
especially I hate to admit, MIT, provided new entrepreneurial energy

00:06:21.100 --> 00:06:22.270
for the region.

00:06:22.270 --> 00:06:25.390
Boston may not have the great palaces or monuments

00:06:25.390 --> 00:06:29.890
of imperial cities like Vienna or Paris, but it remains a handsome city,

00:06:29.890 --> 00:06:32.350
displaying the wealth that its merchants earned by using

00:06:32.350 --> 00:06:35.280
their wits to trade with the world.

